+## 🔵 ACTION FOR HANK — W10 fixed in the harness; the *binding* half needs two commands from you
+
+**Two minutes of your time, once. Nothing is blocked until you do it — but until you
+do, the top item in the RESOLVED block below ("the exposure is the *next* flip, not
+this one") is still true.**
+
+### What changed, and what it already buys
+
+`evals/labeled_set.yaml`, `.claude/approval/**` and `.claude/scripts/approval_gate.py`
+are now `harness_lib.HUMAN_ONLY`. That list is checked in three places, and only the
+first is the one people usually mean by "the guard":
+
+| where | before | now |
+|---|---|---|
+| Edit/Write guard, no claim held | ALLOW | DENY |
+| Edit/Write guard, holding T-7 (scope `evals/**`) | ALLOW | DENY |
+| `claim.sh close` integrity, file written via Bash mid-ticket | rc 0, receipt minted | rc 2, refused by name |
+| `claim.sh claim`, file written via Bash *before* claiming | rc 0, absorbed into the start commit | rc 1, refused |
+
+All four were reproduced against the shipped harness first. The third and fourth rows
+are the ones that matter: **the `Bash` tool is matched by no PreToolUse hook**
+(`.claude/settings.json` has exactly two matchers, `Edit|Write|NotebookEdit` and
+`TaskUpdate`), so `python3 -c` and `sed -i` can still *write* these files — including
+the way you yourself flipped the block. What they can no longer do is finish a ticket
+afterwards. That is the harness's own documented compensating control for the Bash
+hole (protocol rule 4), applied to the one file it had been leaving out.
+
+`evals/**` is now `PROTECTED` as well, which closes W10's other half — a session
+holding *no* claim was unconstrained for every non-PROTECTED path, so it could rewrite
+the eval corpus and `report.py` freely. PROTECTED yields to an explicit scope grant, so
+**T-7, T-15, T-21 and T-25 are unaffected** (verified case by case, T-21 included —
+it is in flight).
+
+### What still needs you: arm the signature anchor
+
+Right now nothing in this repo can tell your approval from an agent-written one — the
+verify reads three strings any agent can type. `.claude/scripts/approval_gate.py` fixes
+that by requiring a detached SSH signature over the labels *and* the approval fields,
+made with a key whose passphrase exists only in your head. It ships **unarmed**: with
+no key in the anchor it reports and refuses nothing, so nothing has changed for you yet.
+
+```sh
+# 1. a signing key — passphrase-protected, and never `ssh-add` it
+ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C 'cxforge label approval' -f ~/.ssh/cxforge_approval
+
+# 2. record its public half as the trust anchor
+printf 'hank@cxforge %s\n' "$(cut -d' ' -f1,2 ~/.ssh/cxforge_approval.pub)" \
+  >> .claude/approval/allowed_signers
+```
+
+Then, each time you approve, three more commands — see `.claude/approval/README.md`.
+Once armed, any ticket whose verify *reads* the approval (T-7's does) is refused at
+close while that approval is unsignable. **Do not load the key into ssh-agent:** your
+launchd agent is reachable from the agent's own environment, and a key it holds is a
+key the agent can sign with, silently — measured, not assumed.
+
+Why this and not commit signing: an agent here already produced a `%G? = G`,
+`Good signature` commit with no prompt and no touch, using the agent-held ssh-agent
+key. A passphrase is the only zero-install secret on this machine that an unattended
+agent cannot supply — verified: with the private key file fully readable and no
+passphrase, `ssh-keygen -Y sign` fails and writes nothing.
+
+### What this does NOT stop — please read this part
+
+1. **The anchor is a file in a repo the agent can write.** An agent with Bash can
+   append its own key to `allowed_signers` and sign with it, and the result genuinely
+   verifies. What it cannot do is close the ticket: the anchor is HUMAN_ONLY, so the
+   close names it and refuses (there is a test for exactly this). **Tamper-evident and
+   close-blocking, not impossible.** Nothing living inside a repo the agent can write
+   can be more than that. Stronger needs a hardware touch — `brew install libfido2` +
+   `SSH_SK_PROVIDER` against the YubiKey already plugged in — which is an install and
+   your call.
+2. **Between tickets there is no window.** A write with no claim open, never followed
+   by a close, is checked by nothing. That is W12's gap and it is untouched here.
+3. **Your own workflow changes slightly.** Your sign-off must land as its own commit
+   with no claim open. If you edit it while a ticket is open, that ticket's close is
+   refused — deliberately, so your approval can never be laundered through an agent's
+   attested diff. The refusal explains the remedy (release → commit it alone →
+   re-claim) and explicitly tells the session **not** to `git reset --hard`, which
+   would delete your sign-off.
+
+### Two things found on the way, neither fixed here
+
+* **`uv run mypy backend` already fails at HEAD** — 2 errors, in
+  `backend/tests/plan/update_structural_snapshot.py:30` and
+  `test_structural_snapshot.py:89`, both predating this change. T-0's verify runs
