+# The label-approval gate — arming it, and what it is worth
+
+`evals/labeled_set.yaml`'s `approval:` block is the one gate the plan reserves for
+you (docs/tickets.json, T-7 non_goal: *"No synthetic label approval — human
+sign-off is external ground truth"*).
+
+Today that gate is three strings. T-7's verify reads exactly those three strings:
+
+```
+a['status']=='APPROVED' and a['approved_by'] and a['approved_date']
+```
+
+You typed them yourself — that is settled and not in question. The problem is that
+**the repo cannot tell that from the alternative.** An agent typing `"Hank
+Holcomb"` and today's date produces a byte-identical pass and a byte-identical
+receipt. The exposure is the *next* flip, not the last one.
+
+## Arming it (two commands, once)
+
+**1. Make a signing key. Passphrase-protected, and never `ssh-add` it.**
+
+```sh
+ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C 'cxforge label approval' -f ~/.ssh/cxforge_approval
+```
+
+Choose a passphrase you do not store anywhere and do not type into an agent
+session. That passphrase is the entire mechanism. The agent runs as your uid and
+can *read* `~/.ssh/cxforge_approval` — verified. It cannot *use* it: with the key
+file fully readable and no passphrase, `ssh-keygen -Y sign` fails with
+`incorrect passphrase supplied` and writes nothing. Read access is not signing
+capability. Do **not** load it into ssh-agent: your launchd agent is reachable
+from the agent's own environment, and a key it holds is a key the agent can sign
+with, silently, in one Bash call.
+
+**2. Record the public half as the anchor.**
+
+```sh
+printf 'hank@cxforge %s\n' "$(cut -d' ' -f1,2 ~/.ssh/cxforge_approval.pub)" \
+  >> .claude/approval/allowed_signers
+```
+
+## Signing an approval (each time you approve)
+
+Set the three fields in `evals/labeled_set.yaml` as you do now, then:
+
+```sh
+python3 .claude/scripts/approval_gate.py payload > /tmp/cxforge-approval.txt
+cat /tmp/cxforge-approval.txt          # read it — this is what you are attesting
+ssh-keygen -Y sign -f ~/.ssh/cxforge_approval -n cxforge-label-approval \
+  /tmp/cxforge-approval.txt
+mv /tmp/cxforge-approval.txt.sig .claude/approval/labeled_set.sig
+python3 .claude/scripts/approval_gate.py verify    # expect exit 0
+```
+
+Commit the approval **as its own commit, with no ticket claim open.** The harness
+refuses to absorb a change to these files into any ticket's attested range — in
+either direction, at claim time and at close time — precisely so your sign-off can
+never be laundered through an agent's diff.
+
+The payload covers the labels *and* the three approval fields. Re-label a ticket
+after signing, or change the name or date, and the old signature stops verifying.
+That is intended: the signature attests to a specific set of labels, not to the
+file's name.
+
+## What this is worth, stated honestly
+
+**Binding.** Producing a valid signature requires the passphrase. Nothing on this
+machine can supply it: gpg is not installed, the attached YubiKey has no FIDO
+middleware (`ssh-keygen -t ecdsa-sk` → *"No FIDO SecurityKeyProvider specified"*),
+and the ssh-agent key is deliberately not this one. An unattended agent — the
+actual threat here, two hours of drift with nobody watching — cannot construct the
+artifact at all, as opposed to being merely forbidden to.
+
+**Not binding.** The anchor is a file in a repo the agent can write.
+
+- `.claude/approval/**` and `.claude/scripts/approval_gate.py` are
+  `harness_lib.HUMAN_ONLY`: Edit/Write denied under **every** claim including one
+  whose scope names them, and any ticket window that changes them **fails the
+  close-time integrity check**.
+- The `Bash` tool is matched by no PreToolUse hook. A `python3 -c` or `sed -i` can
+  still write any of these files. What it cannot do is finish the ticket
+  afterwards: the close names the file and refuses.
+- Between tickets, with no claim open and nothing subsequently closed, there is no
+  window and therefore no check. That is the same gap that voided T-27/T-28's
+  receipts (W12) and it is not fixed here.
+
+So: **an agent cannot forge an approval that verifies. It can still forge one that
+does not verify, and it can still substitute the anchor — and both of those now
+leave a close-blocking, named diff instead of a clean receipt.** Detection and
+cost, not impossibility, for the anchor; genuine structural incapability for the
+signature itself. Anything stronger needs a hardware token with a physical touch
+(`brew install libfido2` + `SSH_SK_PROVIDER`, using the YubiKey already attached),
+which is an install, not a config change, and is your call to make.
